articles=[
('active-recall','Active recall: how to remember what you study','Active recall means trying to bring an idea to mind before looking at the answer.','Active recall is a way to study by retrieving information from memory. Instead of rereading a page first, close the notes and try to explain, list, draw, or answer questions about what you learned. Then check the result and correct gaps.',[
('Why does active recall help?','Trying to retrieve an idea shows what you can explain and what still needs work. It turns revision into a check of learning rather than familiarity. A wrong or incomplete answer is useful information: reopen a reliable source, correct the gap, and try again later.'),('How can I try it?','Choose a small topic. Review it for understanding, hide the material, and write or say what you remember. Compare your answer with a reliable source, correct errors, and return to the topic after some time.'),('What is an example?','After studying a process, close the book and draw its stages from memory. Compare labels and connections with the source, then correct the diagram. Feedback matters because recall without correction can leave misunderstandings in place.'),('When should I use another approach?','If a topic is new, begin with an explanation or worked example. Then use retrieval to check what you learned. For complex skills, combine recall with practice problems and feedback.')], [('Is active recall the same as rereading?','No. Rereading exposes you to the material; active recall asks you to bring an answer to mind before checking.'),('What if I cannot remember?','Review a smaller section, use a hint, and try again after you understand the material.')], [('Dunlosky et al. (2013), Improving Students’ Learning With Effective Learning Techniques','https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2013.01065.x'),('The Learning Scientists: Retrieval Practice','https://www.learningscientists.org/retrieval-practice')]),
('spaced-practice','Spaced practice: how to spread out your revision','Spaced practice means returning to learning over separate sessions instead of cramming it all at once.','Spaced practice means revisiting material across multiple sessions with time between them. It can help you remember learning for longer than one concentrated session, especially when you try to retrieve the material before checking it. There is no single perfect schedule for everyone.',[
('How do I make a simple plan?','List the topics you need to learn. Put a short review on your calendar on more than one day. Begin with a manageable gap, then adjust: revisit sooner when recall is difficult and leave longer when you can explain the topic accurately.'),('What should I do in each review?','Start by recalling the topic from memory, then check your notes or answer key. Work a fresh problem for a skill. Record mistakes and uncertain points for the next review.'),('Is there a perfect number of days between sessions?','No. The useful interval depends on the deadline, difficulty, how well you can retrieve it, and your available time. Treat a schedule as an adaptable plan.'),('What if an exam is soon?','Prioritize important topics and mix short reviews with practice questions. Avoid spending all your time making a timetable.')], [('Is spaced practice just studying less?','No. It changes when you return to the material; each session still needs focused work and feedback.'),('Should I space every topic equally?','No. Give more attention to topics that remain difficult while returning to all important material over time.')], [('Dunlosky et al. (2013), Improving Students’ Learning With Effective Learning Techniques','https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2013.01065.x'),('The Learning Scientists: Spaced Practice','https://www.learningscientists.org/spaced-practice')]),
('flashcards','How to make flashcards that help you learn','A useful flashcard asks one focused question and gives a clear answer you can check.','Flashcards are most useful when you use them to retrieve an answer, then check whether it is correct. They are not a substitute for understanding a topic or practising a skill. Keep each card focused enough that you can judge whether an answer is correct.',[
('What makes a good card?','Write a prompt about one idea, relationship, definition, or step. Keep the answer short but complete enough to check. Avoid copying a whole paragraph onto the back.'),('How should I review a deck?','Try to answer without looking, then check. Sort cards by accurate, partly correct, and not yet. Revisit difficult cards after a break and return to the topic later.'),('Are flashcards right for every subject?','No. Cards suit facts, terms, steps, and checkable answers. For essays, calculations, and complex problem solving, use practice tasks and feedback too.'),('How do I avoid memorizing without understanding?','Ask why ideas connect, explain answers in your own words, and apply them to a new example. Return to the lesson when you miss a card.')], [('How many cards should I make?','Make enough to cover useful learning goals. A focused deck is easier to review than copied sentences.'),('Handmade or app?','Use a format you can access and review consistently. The learning action is attempting an answer and checking it.')], [('Dunlosky et al. (2013), Improving Students’ Learning With Effective Learning Techniques','https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2013.01065.x'),('The Learning Scientists: Retrieval Practice','https://www.learningscientists.org/retrieval-practice')]),
('revision-plan','How to make a realistic revision plan','A useful revision plan turns a list of topics into short sessions you can actually complete.','A revision plan is a working schedule, not a promise to study every hour. Start with the topics and deadlines you have, choose small tasks, and leave room to adjust.',[
('1. List topics and deadlines','Write each subject, the topics you need to cover, and the dates that matter. Check the list against class materials.'),('2. Choose a useful task','Replace “study biology” with an action: explain a process from memory, solve three problems, or review errors. Give the task a clear finish point.'),('3. Prioritize and schedule','Give earlier attention to important, unfamiliar, or difficult topics. Spread topics across days when possible. Keep sessions realistic for school, home, and rest.'),('4. Review the plan','Note what you could do and what needs another attempt. Move unfinished tasks rather than filling every remaining hour. Ask a teacher for help prioritizing if needed.')], [('How long should a session be?','Choose a length that fits your attention, task, and schedule. Try a short block and adjust based on how well you work.'),('Should I include breaks?','Yes. Include breaks and normal rest in a schedule you can sustain.')], [('Education Endowment Foundation: Metacognition and Self-Regulated Learning','https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/education-evidence/guidance-reports/metacognition')]),
('learn-from-mistakes','How to learn from practice test mistakes','Reviewing a mistake works best when you identify why it happened and try a corrected problem.','A practice test is useful when it changes what you do next. Identify the kind of error, check the correct reasoning, and try a similar task after you understand the correction.',[
('What kind of mistake did I make?','Was a fact missing? Did you misunderstand the question, choose the wrong method, make a calculation slip, or run out of time? Naming the error helps you choose a response.'),('How do I correct it?','Return to a trustworthy explanation or ask your teacher. Write the correct steps in your own words. Close the solution and try the question again.'),('How do I check that I learned from it?','Try a related but not identical question later. Explain why the method fits and complete the new task.'),('What should I record?','Keep a short error log: topic, error type, correction, and next action. Do not treat one practice score as a prediction of a final grade.')], [('Should I redo it straight away?','First understand the correction. Then redo it without looking and later try a similar question.'),('Does one low practice score mean I will fail?','A practice result is one piece of information about preparation under particular conditions. Use errors to guide study and ask a teacher for help.')], [('Education Endowment Foundation: Metacognition and Self-Regulated Learning','https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/education-evidence/guidance-reports/metacognition'),('Dunlosky et al. (2013)','https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2013.01065.x')])]

CSS=''':root{--ink:#142b3b;--muted:#526574;--paper:#f7f7f2;--teal:#126d68;--lime:#d5eb82;--line:#dde4dd;--coral:#fa8a68}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;color:var(--ink);background:var(--paper);font:16px/1.65 Arial,'Segoe UI',sans-serif}a{color:var(--teal);text-underline-offset:3px}a:focus-visible,button:focus-visible,input:focus-visible{outline:3px solid var(--coral);outline-offset:3px}.wrap{width:min(1120px,calc(100% - 40px));margin:auto}.skip{position:absolute;left:-999px;top:8px;background:white;padding:10px;z-index:9}.skip:focus{left:8px}.topbar{border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}header{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:24px;padding:18px 0}.brand{font:800 1.22rem Arial,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;color:var(--ink);text-decoration:none}.brand b{color:var(--teal)}nav{display:flex;gap:26px}nav a{font-size:.93rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);text-decoration:none}.hero{padding:70px 0 48px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.3fr .7fr;gap:45px;align-items:center}.eyebrow{font-size:.76rem;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.12em;color:var(--teal)}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Arial,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;line-height:1.16;letter-spacing:-.035em}h1{font-size:clamp(2.5rem,6vw,4.4rem);margin:.55rem 0 1rem}h2{font-size:clamp(1.65rem,3vw,2.4rem)}.lead{font-size:1.18rem;max-width:650px;color:var(--muted)}.hero-card{background:var(--lime);border-radius:24px;padding:28px}.hero-card p{font:700 1.55rem/1.3 Arial}.button{display:inline-block;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--teal);color:white;border-radius:999px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700}.section{padding:38px 0 62px}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:18px}.card{padding:23px;border-radius:18px;background:white;border:1px solid var(--line);min-height:205px}.card h3{font-size:1.18rem}.card p{color:var(--muted)}.card a{font-weight:700}.tag{font-size:.72rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.1em;color:var(--teal);font-weight:700}.callout,.answer{background:#eaf1e2;padding:18px 22px;border-left:4px solid var(--teal);border-radius:0 12px 12px 0}.searchbox{display:flex;gap:10px;margin-top:24px;max-width:620px}.searchbox input{flex:1;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:14px;font:inherit}.searchbox button{border:0;border-radius:12px;padding:0 18px;background:var(--ink);color:white;font-weight:700}.article{max-width:800px;padding:24px 0 70px}.crumbs{padding-top:22px;color:var(--muted)}.article h1{font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.5rem)}.article h2{font-size:1.6rem;margin-top:38px}.article-meta,.notice{font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted)}.answer{font-size:1.2rem}.related{display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap}.related a{background:white;border:1px solid var(--line);padding:8px 12px;border-radius:999px;text-decoration:none}.faq{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding:15px 0}.faq h3{font-size:1.1rem}.footer{background:var(--ink);color:#eaf0ed;padding:40px 0}.footer a{color:var(--lime)}.footer p{color:#c7d3d5}.footergrid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:25px}@media(max-width:760px){.hero{grid-template-columns:1fr;padding-top:45px}.cards{grid-template-columns:1fr}.footergrid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.searchbox{flex-direction:column}.searchbox button{padding:13px}}'''
(S/'assets').mkdir(exist_ok=True); (S/'assets/site.css').write_text(CSS,encoding='utf-8')
(S/'assets/site.js').write_text("const i=document.querySelector('[data-search]');const form=document.querySelector('[data-search-form]');const cards=[...document.querySelectorAll('[data-card]')];const status=document.querySelector('[data-results]');function run(){const q=i.value.trim().toLowerCase();let n=0;cards.forEach(c=>{c.hidden=!!q&&!c.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(q);if(!c.hidden)n++});if(status)status.textContent=q?`${n} guide${n===1?'':'s'} found`:`Showing all ${n} guides`;}if(i)i.addEventListener('input',run);if(form)form.addEventListener('submit',e=>{e.preventDefault();run()});",encoding='utf-8')
ORG={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Organization','@id':'https://studyready.example/#organization','name':'StudyReady','url':'https://studyready.example/','description':'Illustrative study-skills resource prototype.'}
def shell(title,desc,body,canonical,extra=''):
 return f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{title} | StudyReady</title><meta name="description" content="{desc}"><link rel="canonical" href="https://studyready.example/{canonical}"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css"><meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f7f2">{extra}</head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><div class="topbar"><div class="wrap"><header><a class="brand" href="/">study<b>ready</b></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><a href="/about.html">About</a></nav></header></div></div>{body}<footer class="footer"><div class="wrap footergrid"><div><a class="brand" href="/">study<b>ready</b></a><p>Clear study guidance students can try today, with the reasoning and sources visible.</p></div><div><strong>Explore</strong><p><a href="/guides/">All guides</a> · <a href="/about.html">Editorial approach</a></p><p>Prototype; educator review required before public release.</p></div></div></footer><script src="/assets/site.js" defer></script></body></html>'''
cards=''.join(f'<article class="card" data-card><div class="tag">Study guide</div><h3><a href="/guides/{a[0]}.html">{a[1]}</a></h3><p>{a[2]}</p><a href="/guides/{a[0]}.html">Read the guide →</a></article>' for a in articles)
home=f'''<main id="main"><section class="wrap hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">Study skills, made usable</div><h1>Learn a little.<br>Remember more.</h1><p class="lead">Clear, practical guides to help you revise, plan, and check what you understand—one small study task at a time.</p><a class="button" href="/guides/">Explore the guides</a><form class="searchbox" data-search-form role="search"><label class="skip" for="guide-search">Search guides</label><input id="guide-search" data-search type="search" placeholder="Search a study question"><button type="submit">Find a guide</button><p data-results aria-live="polite"></p></form></div><aside class="hero-card"><small>Start with one question</small><p>Can I explain the idea without looking at my notes?</p><span>Try a short recall check, then compare and correct.</span></aside></section><section class="wrap section"><div class="eyebrow">A useful next step</div><h2>Study with a purpose</h2><p class="lead">Each guide begins with a direct answer, then offers steps, examples, and a way to check understanding.</p><div class="cards">{cards}</div></section><section class="wrap section"><div class="callout"><strong>How to use this site</strong><p>Pick one topic, try a small action, and note what you still need to learn. These guides are general study support, not a replacement for a teacher’s instructions or curriculum.</p></div></section></main>'''
(S/'index.html').write_text(shell('Study skills for students','Practical guides on active recall, spaced practice, flashcards, revision planning, and learning from mistakes.',home,'','<script type="application/ld+json">'+json.dumps(ORG)+'</script>'),encoding='utf-8')
for a in articles:
 slug,title,short,intro,sections,faqs,sources=a
 crumb={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'BreadcrumbList','itemListElement':[{'@type':'ListItem','position':1,'name':'Home','item':'https://studyready.example/'},{'@type':'ListItem','position':2,'name':'Guides','item':'https://studyready.example/guides/'},{'@type':'ListItem','position':3,'name':title,'item':f'https://studyready.example/guides/{slug}.html'}]}
 art={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Article','headline':title,'description':short,'mainEntityOfPage':f'https://studyready.example/guides/{slug}.html','publisher':{'@id':'https://studyready.example/#organization'},'author':{'@type':'Organization','name':'StudyReady Editorial Team'},'dateCreated':'2026-11-22','inLanguage':'en'}
 sectionshtml=''.join(f'<section><h2>{h}</h2><p>{t}</p></section>' for h,t in sections)
 faqhtml=''.join(f'<section class="faq"><h3>{q}</h3><p>{ans}</p></section>' for q,ans in faqs)
 src=''.join(f'<li><a href="{u}" rel="noopener">{n}</a></li>' for n,u in sources)
 rel=''.join(f'<a href="{b[0]}.html">{b[1].split(":")[0]}</a>' for b in articles if b[0]!=slug)
 body=f'''<main id="main" class="wrap"><div class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / {title}</div><article class="article"><div class="eyebrow">Study guide</div><h1>{title}</h1><p class="article-meta">StudyReady Editorial Team · Draft prepared 22 November 2026 · General secondary-school study support</p><p class="answer">{short}</p><p>{intro}</p>{sectionshtml}<h2>Common questions</h2>{faqhtml}<h2>Further reading</h2><ul>{src}</ul><p class="notice">Portfolio prototype, not curriculum-approved teaching material. Educator review and source-link checks are required before publication.</p><h2>Related guides</h2><div class="related">{rel}</div></article></main>'''
 (G/f'{slug}.html').write_text(shell(title,short,body,f'guides/{slug}.html','<script type="application/ld+json">'+json.dumps([art,crumb],ensure_ascii=False)+'</script>'),encoding='utf-8')
hub=f'<main id="main" class="wrap section"><div class="eyebrow">Library</div><h1>Study guides</h1><p class="lead">Start with the question you have. Each guide offers one practical approach and a way to check your understanding.</p><h2>All study guides</h2><div class="cards">{cards}</div></main>'
(G/'index.html').write_text(shell('Study guides','Browse StudyReady guides for secondary-school study skills.',hub,'guides/'),encoding='utf-8')
about='''<main id="main" class="wrap article"><div class="eyebrow">About this project</div><h1>Study support with the reasoning in view</h1><p class="lead">StudyReady is a portfolio prototype for a free study-skills library for secondary-school students.</p><h2>Who it is for</h2><p>The initial concept serves learners around ages 13–18, plus caregivers and educators. It is English-first and geography-neutral. No curriculum or accreditation is claimed.</p><h2>Editorial standards</h2><ul><li>Name real authors and qualified reviewers on a public site.</li><li>Link primary research or established education guidance for learning claims.</li><li>Explain what is general guidance and what depends on a curriculum or learner.</li><li>Provide a correction route and dated review record.</li><li>Keep learning information accessible in page text, not only images or downloads.</li></ul><h2>Review status</h2><p>This prototype has not been reviewed by an educator or tested with students. Before publication, assign a content owner, review each claim and link, and provide a contact route for corrections.</p></main>'''
(S/'about.html').write_text(shell('About StudyReady','Purpose and editorial standards of the StudyReady project prototype.',about,'about.html','<script type="application/ld+json">'+json.dumps(ORG)+'</script>'),encoding='utf-8')
(S/'robots.txt').write_text('User-agent: *\nAllow: /\nSitemap: https://studyready.example/sitemap.xml\n',encoding='utf-8')
urls=['https://studyready.example/','https://studyready.example/guides/','https://studyready.example/about.html']+[f'https://studyready.example/guides/{a[0]}.html' for a in articles]
(S/'sitemap.xml').write_text('<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">\n'+''.join(f'<url><loc>{u}</loc><lastmod>2026-11-22</lastmod></url>\n' for u in urls)+'</urlset>\n',encoding='utf-8')

# Question research hypotheses; no search metrics are fabricated.
groups={
'Active recall':['What is active recall?','How do I use active recall to study?','Is active recall better than rereading?','How do I test myself without notes?','What if I cannot remember the answer?','How can I use active recall for science?'],
'Spaced practice':['What is spaced practice?','How should I space out revision?','How many days apart should I revise?','Is spaced repetition the same as spaced practice?','How do I make a spaced revision timetable?'],
'Flashcards':['How do I make good flashcards?','How many flashcards should I make?','How should I use flashcards to revise?','Are flashcards good for every subject?','Should flashcards have one question each?'],
'Revision planning':['How do I make a revision timetable?','How long should a study session be?','How do I revise for multiple exams?','What should I do first when I start revising?','How do I stick to a revision plan?','Should I include breaks in my timetable?'],
'Practice and feedback':['How do I learn from practice test mistakes?','Should I redo questions I got wrong?','How do I review a practice test?','What should I do when stuck on a question?','How do I know if I understand a topic?'],
'Notes and understanding':['How do I take useful study notes?','How do I summarize a topic in my own words?','How do I remember what I read?','Should I rewrite notes to revise?'],
'Audience and context':['How can parents help a teenager revise?','How can I prepare for an exam in one week?','How can I study when I have little time?','How do I balance revision with other commitments?']}
urlmap={'Active recall':'active-recall','Spaced practice':'spaced-practice','Flashcards':'flashcards','Revision planning':'revision-plan','Practice and feedback':'learn-from-mistakes','Notes and understanding':'guides','Audience and context':'guides'}
qrows=[]; irows=[]
for cluster,qs in groups.items():
 for q in qs:
  fmt={'Active recall':'Definition / how-to','Spaced practice':'Definition / planning guide','Flashcards':'How-to / comparison','Revision planning':'Planning guide / checklist','Practice and feedback':'Troubleshooting / worked example','Notes and understanding':'How-to / comparison','Audience and context':'Audience-specific guide'}[cluster]
  qrows.append([q,cluster,'Informational hypothesis',fmt,'Strategic priority; not volume-based','Not measured','Seed hypothesis; validate with audience research and Search Console','Interview; autocomplete/related searches; Trends; Search Console after launch'])
  low=q.lower(); intent='Definition' if low.startswith('what is') else ('Comparison' if any(w in low for w in ('better than','same as','every subject')) else 'How-to / troubleshooting')
  destination='/guides/' if urlmap[cluster]=='guides' else '/guides/'+urlmap[cluster]+'.html'
  irows.append([q,intent,'Understand a concept or complete a study task',fmt,destination,'SERP not collected; live access unavailable','Low pending interviews and live SERP review'])
with (D/'question_keyword_seed.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:
 w=csv.writer(f);w.writerow(['Question / query hypothesis','Topic cluster','Intent hypothesis','Likely page format','Priority basis','Demand / volume','Evidence status','Next validation']);w.writerows(qrows)
with (D/'search_intent_map.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:
 w=csv.writer(f);w.writerow(['Question','Primary intent','Expected outcome','Recommended format','URL candidate','SERP evidence','Confidence']);w.writerows(irows)
